config BCM_4345C5_RFKILL
	tristate "Bluetooth power control driver for BCM-4345C5 module"
	depends on RFKILL && (MMC_SDHCI_INGENIC || INGENIC_MMC_MMC0 || INGENIC_MMC_MMC1)
	default n
	help
	Creates an rfkill entry in sysfs for power control of Bluetooth
	bcm-xxxx chips.

config NEBULAOS_BACKLIGHT_FINAL_CONTROLLER
	bool "NebulaOS kernel-owned backlight final controller (GPC0/PWM0/PC22)"
	depends on PWM
	depends on BACKLIGHT_CLASS_DEVICE
	default n
	help
	  Post-incident redesign (2026-08-02+) of the backlight hardware
	  discovery work - see this driver's own file header
	  (nebulaos_backlight_final_controller.c) for the full incident
	  writeup and design rationale, and build-work/display-analysis/
	  backlight-path-analysis.txt for the underlying candidate-hardware
	  evidence trail.

	  A single kernel-owned platform driver that is the EXCLUSIVE owner
	  of three candidate backlight hardware resources: GPC0 as a plain
	  GPIO, GPC0 muxed to PWM channel 0's peripheral function, and the
	  candidate enable-GPIO PC22. Structurally prevents the exact
	  incident this redesign follows: probe() NEVER acquires any of the
	  three resources (no devm_gpiod_get()/devm_pwm_get()/
	  pinctrl_select_state() calls outside the explicit runtime state-
	  transition functions), and the shared &pwm controller node's own
	  pinctrl-0 is never touched - GPC0's PWM-function pinmux is instead
	  selected dynamically, only by this driver, only at the moment
	  "pwm-active" is actually entered, via this driver's OWN DT node's
	  pinctrl-names/pinctrl-0 (a state deliberately never named "default"
	  or "init", so the device core's automatic pinctrl_bind_pins() never
	  selects it either - see the driver's file header for the exact
	  pinctrl-ingenic.c/pinctrl core call chain this relies on).

	  Explicit, mutex-serialized state machine: boot-preserve (the only
	  state reachable at boot - zero hardware claimed, bootloader's own
	  GPC0 configuration left completely untouched) -> safe-on (GPC0
	  GPIO output driven HIGH - the live-proven-safe restoration target)
	  -> safe-off-test / pwm-active (both bounded, both only reachable
	  FROM safe-on, both always converge back to safe-on on completion,
	  timeout, error, or explicit disarm). A kernel workqueue watchdog is
	  armed BEFORE every hardware mutation (same ordering already proven
	  correct by CONFIG_NEBULAOS_BACKLIGHT_PROBE_DIAG), independent of
	  any userspace process's lifetime, with a hard 2-second cap on any
	  single operation. Never uses legacy /sys/class/gpio sysfs for
	  anything.

	  Disabled by default - never enable for a production/active-slot
	  build until live hardware testing confirms correct behavior. If
	  unsure, say N.
